Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 04:58:46 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 5/6] feat: global `-C <dir>` with Git's meaning

`brit -C <dir>` runs as if brit was started in `<dir>`: repository discovery and
every relative path argument start there. It is repeatable, each directory relative
to the previous one, and an empty value is ignored, as in Git. All `-C` options are
applied first; `-r/--repository` is then resolved relative to the resulting
directory, whatever their order on the command line, the way Git resolves
`--git-dir` after `-C`. `-r` alone still selects only the repository and moves no
relative path.

The journey proves it from an unrelated directory in a normal repository (including
`worktree add ../linked` landing beside the repository), in the linked worktree that
creates, and from a superproject into a submodule, comparing with `git -C`.

